{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And I will turn to you, etc.</b> The verse's saying <b>וּפָנִיתִי</b> is on the way the Sages, whose memory is for blessing, said (Ta'anit 2a) that three keys are in the hand of the Holy One, blessed be He, and one of them is the key of childbirth. Therefore when He wished to say ״וְהִפְרֵיתִי אֶתְכֶם״ He said ״וּפָנִיתִי״ — that He will turn Himself, as it were, to do this thing Himself and not through an agent. Or perhaps it also intends in the statement <b>וְהִרְבֵּיתִי</b> [to refer to] the key of sustenance, for through sustenance their stature grows, and they said in Torat Kohanim: ״וְהִרְבֵּיתִי אֶתְכֶם״ — in upright stature. And in the statement <b>וַהֲקִימֹתִי אֶת בְּרִיתִי</b>, on the way the Sages, whose memory is for blessing, said (Sanhedrin 90b) that a covenant was sealed for those who dwell in the dust to revive them, and it is the third key that is in the hand of the Holy One, blessed be He. And corresponding to all three of them He said ״וּפָנִיתִי״ etc." "<b>Further,</b> it may be explained on the way the Sages, whose memory is for blessing, said (Shabbat 156a): Israel has no mazal, and on the way the verse says (Isaiah 41:2): ״צֶדֶק יִקְרָאֵהוּ לְרַגְלוֹ״ — that HaShem uprooted this mazal from one place and placed it in another place. And that is the meaning of HaShem's saying ״וּפָנִיתִי אֲלֵיכֶם וְהִפְרֵיתִי״ etc., corresponding to children, life, and sustenance — even though all three depend on mazal and not on merit (Mo'ed Katan 28a), I will turn the mazal from place to place, directing it toward you to be fruitful; this corresponds to children. <b>וְהִרְבֵּיתִי אֶתְכֶם</b> corresponds to life, on the way the verse says (Deuteronomy 11:21): ״לְמַעַן יִרְבּוּ יְמֵיכֶם״ etc. <b>וַהֲקִימֹתִי אֶת בְּרִיתִי</b> corresponds to sustenance, and the Name attributes the term ברית to it on the way the verse says (Genesis 8:21): ״וַיֹּאמֶר ה׳ אֶל לִבּוֹ לֹא אֹסִף״ etc. ״עוֹד כָּל יְמֵי הָאָרֶץ זֶרַע וְקָצִיר״ etc., and the Sages, whose memory is for blessing, said (Shavuot 36a) that what He said ״לֹא אֹסִף״ is an oath, and it is written there (Genesis 9:12): ״זֹאת אוֹת הַבְּרִית״, and everything stated there is included under the general category of the oath. Examine closely His statement here, that He will establish with them that covenant of seed and harvest, and that is His saying <b>וַהֲקִימֹתִי אֶת בְּרִיתִי אִתְּכֶם</b> — for the oath was not stated in any general way over the entire earth, for behold there are many years when there is no seed and harvest in part of the earth, since HaShem did not say there, in the covenant of Noah, ״יְמֵי כָּל הָאָרֶץ.״ And see what I explained in the verse (Deuteronomy 6:5): ״וְאָהַבְתָּ אֵת ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ״." "<b>Further,</b> it may be explained that His saying <b>וּפָנִיתִי אֲלֵיכֶם</b> is on the way of what the prophet said (II Samuel 6:10–11): ״וְלֹא אָבָה דָוִד לְהָסִיר אֵלָיו אֲרוֹן ה׳״ etc. ״וַיַּטֵּהוּ דָוִד בֵּית עוֹבֵד״ etc. ״וַיְבָרֶךְ ה׳ אֶת עוֹבֵד״ etc., and the Sages, whose memory is for blessing, said (Berakhot 63b): his wife and her eight daughters-in-law gave birth to six in one womb, as it is written (I Chronicles 26:8): ״פְּעֻלְּתַי הַשְּׁמִינִי שִׁשִּׁים וּשְׁנַיִם״ — end of quote. And that is the meaning of HaShem's saying <b>וּפָנִיתִי אֲלֵיכֶם וְהִרְבֵּיתִי אֶתְכֶם</b> — that through the medium of HaShem's turning toward them, the matter will follow of itself to multiply them, just as it followed for the house of Oved-Edom when David turned the ark of HaShem toward him. And the statement <b>וְהִרְבֵּיתִי אֶתְכֶם</b> is in the language of greatness and mastery, and the intention in this is that even though they will be so very numerous — and according to the usual ways of things that exist in the world, everything that multiplies diminishes in its value, as we expanded in explanation of this particular point in other places (Exodus 1:9, Numbers 13:18) — nevertheless ״וְהִרְבֵּיתִי אֶתְכֶם״. And His saying <b>וַהֲקִימֹתִי אֶת בְּרִיתִי אִתְּכֶם</b> — He promised them what the prophet promised, as it is written (Isaiah 59:21): ״וַאֲנִי זֹאת בְּרִיתִי אוֹתָם אָמַר ה׳״ etc. ״לֹא יָמוּשׁוּ מִפִּיךָ״ etc. — that Torah shall not cease from their seed. Further, He also intended to say, in a general sense, a covenant of favor that He will renew the covenant with them beyond the covenant of the fathers, and that is His saying ״וַהֲקִימֹתִי אֶת בְּרִיתִי״ — that which I already swore to your fathers, I will establish with you anew."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:02:04.847984227Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}